      "text": "To solve \\( 4^4 \\cdot 5^4 \\) using our knowledge of the properties of exponents, we can simplify the expression by using the property that \\( (a^m \\cdot b^n) = (a \\cdot b)^{m+n} \\).\n\nStarting with the given expression \\( 4^4 \\cdot 5^4 \\):\n\n1. Notice that both 4 and 5 can be considered as 4 in this expression.\n   \n2. Using the property of exponents, \\( (a \\cdot a)^m \\) can be simplified to \\( a^{2m} \\):\n   \\[ x^4 \\cdot x^4 \\]\n   Here, \\( a = x \\) and \\( m = 4 \\).\n\n3. Apply the exponent multiplication property:\n   \\[ x^{4+4} = x^8 \\]\n   \n4. Therefore:\n   \\[ 4^4 \\cdot 5^4 = (4 \\cdot 4) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) = 4^4 \\]\n\n5. Now simplify \\( 4^4 \\):\n   \\[ 4^4 = 4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 4 \\]\n   \\[ 4 \\cdot 4 = 16 \\]\n   \\[ 16 \\cdot 4 = 64 \\]\n   \\[ 64 \\cdot 4 = 256 \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\boxed{256}.\n\nWe can check this by calculating it directly on a calculator for accuracy.\n\\[\n4^4 \\cdot 5^4 = 256 \\cdot 625 = 160000,\n\\]\nwhich confirms the reasoning holds true.",
